[38;5;231m⏺[39m Project state, which matters for the design: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is loaded as a [1mclassic script[0m ([38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m), not a module —
  there's no bundler, no [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m, no dependencies, and no test runner ([38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts or devDeps).
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are a separate CommonJS Node entry point unrelated to the login page. So "works across
  the app" currently has no module system to work across; that's part of what we'd be deciding.

  The question that determines everything downstream is where the identity actually comes from, because [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub
  ([38;5;153mapp.js:4-8[39m) that never contacts [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m and returns the username the user typed.

  The fork: whether the persisted identity is [1mauthenticated[0m or [1mself-asserted[0m.

  - [1mA real backend authenticates and returns an ID.[0m The stored identity means something — the server vouched for it.
    Costs a defined response contract and an auth story, and the client can't do this until that endpoint exists.
  - [1mClient-only, storing the typed username.[0m Works today with no server. But the "identity" is whatever the user typed
    into a text box — anyone can open devtools and set it to any value. It's fine for a display name or a UI
    convenience, and it is [3mnot[0m something later forms should trust for authorization or attribution.

  I want to be explicit about that second point rather than bury it: if other forms will read this value later, the
  difference between "the server said this is who they are" and "this is what someone typed" is the difference between
  an audit trail and a decoration. Choosing client-only now and hardening later means revisiting every consumer.

  My recommendation depends on your answer, so I'll ask before proposing approaches.
